 "deepDive": {
  "domain": "Economy, business & markets",
  "title": "One boardroom decision swung markets on three continents",
  "body": "**Analysis.** Monday showed how tightly markets in all four places are now tied to a single trade: US AI stocks. Friday's Wall Street rout (Nasdaq −5.3%) arrived in Mumbai before the US reopened. The Sensex fell 1.8%, led by banks and metals, and the rupee briefly passed 93 per dollar. In Warsaw the zloty stayed near a three-year low against the euro after the WIG20 lost 6.5% last week.\n\nThen Nvidia's board brought back Jensen Huang, and the S&P 500 had its best day since April 2025. That one company's governance can move global indices this much is itself the risk. Strategists noted that nothing has changed on the underlying worries: returns on AI spending, a Fed that markets price at about 92–98% to raise rates on Dec 9, and soft consumer demand. Wednesday's PCE inflation figure is the next test.\n\n**Poland** faces a home-grown problem as well. October industry barely grew (+0.1% y/y), construction fell 9.3%, and producer prices rose 8%. Weak output argues for cuts, but factory-gate inflation limits how far the RPP can ease, and two seats on the council change hands this winter. In Kraków, pay is still rising but registered unemployment rose to 2.8% in September (GUS via Manager+), which matters for a city budget that depends on PIT.\n\n**India** also faces tight policy: CPI is above 6% and analysts expect an RBI hike on Dec 4. With Brent near $89, oil remains the main risk for the rupee.\n\n**Trade.** USTR Greer says a US–Canada deal is 'fairly close', but Chinese EVs and steel are the sticking point. The same China-transshipment test applies to the India–US first tranche. India is also using trade as diplomacy: it offered Nepal transit through Kandla port as part of the ₹15,000 crore package.",
